# Getting started

{{owner}} has just created this project, or is coming back to it unsure where to begin.
Walk them through the first session, using `docs/OWNER.md` ("Day one" and "Later, when
needed") as your source, so they do not have to read it themselves.

How to walk them through it:

- **One step at a time.** Say what the step is and why it matters in two or three
  sentences, do the part that is yours, and wait for {{owner}}'s answer before the next
  step.
- **Plain words.** {{owner}} is a mathematician who may be new to agents and terminals.
  Explain a term from `GLOSSARY.md` the first time it comes up, in one sentence.
- **Their decisions stay theirs.** You may explain, propose and do the steps they agree
  to. You never choose for them which question is active.
- Skip any step that is already done, and say so in one line.

## 1. Check the setup

First run `bin/doctor`. It checks the live session, not the files: that this session
started in the project's own folder, that the project's hooks (its automatic checks) ran
for that very command,{{#if harness.is_codex}} that Codex trusts the folder and runs the main thread on the
configured model,{{/if}} and, once there are any, that subagents run as the project's own agents.
For each FAIL, tell {{owner}} in plain words what is not loaded and how to fix it (each
line says how), and stop here until it passes: work done in a session that fails it runs
without the project's reviewers, checks and settings, and nothing else would tell them.
If {{owner}} tells you to go on anyway (the check may misfire on an unusual folder
arrangement), go on, but first say which protections are missing, one per FAIL line, and
log the failure as a setup problem{{#if modules.reflect}} (`bin/journal --add friction "doctor FAIL, continued on {{owner}}'s say-so: <the FAIL lines>"`){{/if}}.
Typical fixes: start the agent again from the project folder (in a terminal, `cd` into it
first; in VS Code, File > Open Folder… and choose the project folder itself, not a folder
above it); on Windows, from Ubuntu rather than the Windows side{{#if harness.is_codex}}; trust the folder when Codex
asks, and type `/hooks` and trust the project's hooks{{/if}}. If the project's path starts with
`/mnt/`, it is on the Windows drive, where it runs slowly; mention that moving it into the
Linux home folder helps.
{{#if harness.is_codex}}

If they use the Codex extension in VS Code, suggest running `codex` in VS Code's built-in
terminal instead: the extension does not load plugins and may skip the hooks.
{{/if}}

Run `bin/selftest`. If every line says PASS, tell them the setup is healthy in one
sentence. If a line says FAIL, run `bin/selftest -v`, explain what is broken, and offer to
fix it before going on.

{{#if modules.questions}}
## 2. The question to start on

Read the files in `wiki/questions/` and summarize each question in one or two plain
sentences, with its status. Explain: agents work only on questions {{owner}} has made
*active*, and only {{owner}} decides that.

{{#if seeds.active_question}}
In the interview {{owner}} chose {{seeds.active_question}} to start on, so it is already
active. Ask whether that is still the one; if they want a different one, see below.
{{else}}
No question is active yet. Ask which one they want to start on, and recommend starting
with one: a single question gives a focused first round and shows how the setup behaves.
If none says what they want answered, offer to draft a new one with them (the
`new-question` skill).
{{/if}}

Only when {{owner}} tells you in this conversation which question to activate, set its
`status: active` and `activated-by: {{owner}}`, then run `bin/question-check` and confirm
it passes. If they are unsure, leave everything as it is: nothing breaks, and they can
activate one later by asking any agent.

If a question file still has a placeholder, "(Not stated yet: …)" under "Why it matters" or
"(Fill in: …)" under "What is known", offer to fill it with them now, starting with the
question they start on. Ask one short question per placeholder and write their answer in
their words; for "What is known", what they know offhand is enough, since the agents add
the literature later: mark it "(from memory, to be checked)", so the first literature search
confirms or corrects it. If they would rather skip it, leave the placeholder.
{{/if}}

{{#if modules.literature}}
## 3. Sources to read first

Read `wiki/inbox/_index.md`. If it lists sources, name them and explain that the agents
read and summarize a source into the project's wiki before working from it. Offer to start
on that now; do it only if {{owner}} agrees. If the inbox is empty, ask whether there are
papers or notes the agents should know about, and offer to add them.
{{/if}}

## 4. How the work goes from here

In a few sentences, explain what a working session looks like:

- {{owner}} says what they want; you propose a concrete step tied to an active question
  and say what it is for before doing anything long.
{{#if modules.gates}}
- Before a finding is recorded, separate reviewer agents check it: whether the conclusion
  follows from the evidence, and whether it survives an attempt to break it.
{{/if}}
- What only {{owner}} does: activating questions, setting priorities, and approving the
  changes to the setup that the reflection loop asks about (`docs/OWNER.md`, "What only
  you do").
- When something in the setup gets in their way, they can just say so: you log it
  (the `friction` skill), and the `reflect` skill later turns recurring problems into
  fixes.{{#if harness.is_codex}} To run a skill themselves, they type `$` and its name (for
  example `$reflect`); `/skills` lists them.{{/if}} Any reminder that annoys them can be switched off (`docs/OWNER.md`, "Switching
  off a nudge").
- Coming back another day: open a terminal, `cd` into this folder, start the agent, and
  ask where things stand. The files hold the state; nothing depends on this session.

Ask whether they have questions about any of it.

## 5. Things that can wait

From "Later, when needed" in `docs/OWNER.md`, mention only the items that apply to this
project, in one line each, and offer to do any of them now:
{{#if modules.lean}}
- where their Lean project lives (`[stack] lake_path` in `project.toml`), needed before
  Lean work;
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.readers}}
- their own papers and a model text for the write-up reviewers
  (`docs/discipline/exposition.md`), if they did not give them in the interview;
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.cache}}
- what the cache of computed results is keyed by (`cache/manifest.md`), when an agent
  first wants to cache a value;
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.commit_gate}}
- `bin/setup-hooks` in any new copy (clone) of this repository;
{{/if}}
- the models and what they cost (`docs/OWNER.md`, "Models" and "What this costs").

## 6. First real step

{{#if modules.questions}}
If a question is active, ask: "Shall I propose a first step on it?" On yes, propose one
concrete step tied to that question, say what it would show, and wait for their go-ahead
before starting.
{{else}}
Ask what they would like to work on first, propose one concrete step, and wait for their
go-ahead before starting.
{{/if}}
